{
  "text": " You've done more, Steve, that for than anybody to really goad the opposition into debating this. And it's been interesting to me, but just as a study in your character about what it is that makes you so relentless that you're a math-based, science-based person. That's what your life is. Engineering, mathematics, and there's a purity in that. And there's kind of a search for empirical truths. And that's the most important part of certain professions, and particularly some professions in the sciences and mathematics. It's supposed to be this unvarnished search for empirical truths, and you get to those truths by allowing them to be challenged in these, and as I said, the furnace of debate. And there was something about you that when you understood this issue, you came to this issue the same way I did, not by having an injured child, which is how most people come to this issue. You came to this issue because you were offended as a science that people who said they were scientists and government agencies who said they were making science-based policies refused to defend the science and could not defend it. And you had this kind of indignation that made you just relentless on this issue. And I love watching, reading your sub-stack, and I love reading your comments, and you're constantly going, you're sending out letters to the FDA. You never get this courage. And you never get cynical, you just keep saying, yeah, you're coming, you're working for me, why aren't you doing your job? And you do it in such a wonderful way. And it's been so, you've been such a gift to this movement, Steve. So I wanted to start out by saying that. Thank you very much.",
